"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[97349],{697349:n=>{n.exports="# SQL Functions — Date & Time\n\nSource dialect date/time functions → DBSQL equivalents. Includes CONVERT style codes and drift warnings.\n\n## MSSQL\n\n### CONVERT style codes\n\n`CONVERT(VARCHAR, col, N)` uses a numeric style code N to control date/time output format. Use `TO_DATE(str, pattern)` when parsing a string into a date; use `DATE_FORMAT(date, pattern)` when formatting a date to a string.\n\n**Pattern differences**: `MM` = month (uppercase), `mm` = minutes; `HH` = 24-hour; `SSS` = milliseconds.\n\n**Standard documented style codes (safe to translate directly):**\n\n| Code | T-SQL Format             | DBSQL `DATE_FORMAT` pattern |\n| ---- | ------------------------ | --------------------------- |\n| 101  | `MM/DD/YYYY` (US)        | `'MM/dd/yyyy'`              |\n| 102  | `YYYY.MM.DD`             | `'yyyy.MM.dd'`              |\n| 103  | `DD/MM/YYYY` (British)   | `'dd/MM/yyyy'`              |\n| 104  | `DD.MM.YYYY` (German)    | `'dd.MM.yyyy'`              |\n| 108  | `HH:MM:SS`               | `'HH:mm:ss'`                |\n| 110  | `MM-DD-YYYY`             | `'MM-dd-yyyy'`              |\n| 112  | `YYYYMMDD` (ISO compact) | `'yyyyMMdd'`                |\n| 120  | `YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS`    | `'yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss'`     |\n| 126  | ISO 8601 with T          | `\"yyyy-MM-dd'T'HH:mm:ss\"`   |\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL: parse string to date (style as third arg to CONVERT)\nCONVERT(DATE, '03/20/2024', 101)\n\n-- DBSQL\nTO_DATE('03/20/2024', 'MM/dd/yyyy')\n\n-- T-SQL: format date to string\nCONVERT(VARCHAR, GETDATE(), 112)\n\n-- DBSQL\nDATE_FORMAT(CURRENT_TIMESTAMP(), 'yyyyMMdd')\n```\n\n**Non-standard or unrecognised codes:** Do NOT guess the meaning. Emit the closest known format with an explicit warning:\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL (style 115 is non-standard)\nSELECT CONVERT(VARCHAR, GETDATE(), 115)\n\n-- DBSQL\nSELECT DATE_FORMAT(CURRENT_TIMESTAMP(), 'yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss')\n-- WARNING: CONVERT style code 115 is non-standard in T-SQL — verify intended format\n```\n\nAny code outside 0–131, or not in the table above, is non-standard. Use `DATE_FORMAT` with the nearest known format and add the warning comment.\n\n### Date function mapping\n\n| T-SQL                        | DBSQL                                                                                    | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| ---------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `GETDATE()`                  | `CURRENT_TIMESTAMP`                                                                      |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| `GETUTCDATE()`               | `UTC_TIMESTAMP()`                                                                        |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| `SYSDATETIME()`              | `CURRENT_TIMESTAMP`                                                                      |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| `DATEADD(day, n, date)`      | `DATEADD(day, n, date)` — **native DBSQL (DBR 10.4+)**; also `DATE_ADD(date, n)`         |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| `DATEADD(month, n, date)`    | `DATEADD(month, n, date)` — **native DBSQL (DBR 10.4+)**; also `ADD_MONTHS(date, n)`     |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| `DATEADD(year, n, date)`     | `DATEADD(year, n, date)` — **native DBSQL (DBR 10.4+)**; also `ADD_MONTHS(date, n*12)`   |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| `DATEADD(hour, n, ts)`       | `DATEADD(hour, n, ts)` — **native DBSQL (DBR 10.4+)**; also `ts + INTERVAL n HOURS`      |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| `DATEADD(minute, n, ts)`     | `DATEADD(minute, n, ts)` — **native DBSQL (DBR 10.4+)**; also `ts + INTERVAL n MINUTES`  |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| `DATEADD(second, n, ts)`     | `DATEADD(second, n, ts)` — **native DBSQL (DBR 10.4+)**; also `ts + INTERVAL n SECONDS`  |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| `DATEADD(week, n, date)`     | `DATEADD(week, n, date)` — **native DBSQL (DBR 10.4+)**; also `DATE_ADD(date, n*7)`      |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| `DATEADD(quarter, n, date)`  | `DATEADD(quarter, n, date)` — **native DBSQL (DBR 10.4+)**; also `ADD_MONTHS(date, n*3)` |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| `DATEDIFF(day, d1, d2)`      | `DATEDIFF(d2, d1)`                                                                       | **Arguments reversed; days only — no unit arg in DBSQL**                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n| `DATEDIFF(year, d1, d2)`     | `YEAR(d2) - YEAR(d1)`                                                                    | **Boundary crossing** (Dec 31 2023 → Jan 1 2024 = 1 year boundary; `YEAR(d2)-YEAR(d1)` gives the same result as T-SQL here)                                                                                                                                     |\n| `DATEDIFF(month, d1, d2)`    | `(YEAR(d2)*12+MONTH(d2)) - (YEAR(d1)*12+MONTH(d1))`                                      | **Boundary crossing**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| `DATEDIFF(quarter, d1, d2)`  | `(YEAR(d2)*4+QUARTER(d2)) - (YEAR(d1)*4+QUARTER(d1))`                                    | **Boundary crossing**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| `DATEDIFF(hour, ts1, ts2)`   | `TIMESTAMPDIFF(HOUR, ts1, ts2)`                                                          | Same argument order as T-SQL                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n| `DATEDIFF(minute, ts1, ts2)` | `TIMESTAMPDIFF(MINUTE, ts1, ts2)`                                                        | Same argument order as T-SQL                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n| `EOMONTH(date)`              | `LAST_DAY(date)`                                                                         | 1-arg form only. See note below for 2-arg `EOMONTH(date, n)`.                                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n| `EOMONTH(date, n)`           | `LAST_DAY(ADD_MONTHS(date, n))`                                                          | T-SQL `EOMONTH` accepts an optional month-offset (e.g. `EOMONTH(dt, 2)` = end of month 2 months forward). `LAST_DAY` is 1-arg only — wrap with `ADD_MONTHS` to handle the offset.                                                                               |\n| `DATEFROMPARTS(y, m, d)`     | `MAKE_DATE(y, m, d)`                                                                     |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| `FORMAT(date, 'fmt')`        | `DATE_FORMAT(date, 'fmt')`                                                               | Format string syntax differs                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n| `ISDATE(str)`                | `TRY_CAST(str AS DATE) IS NOT NULL`                                                      |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| `DATEPART(year, date)`       | `YEAR(date)`                                                                             |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| `DATEPART(month, date)`      | `MONTH(date)`                                                                            |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| `DATEPART(day, date)`        | `DAY(date)`                                                                              |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| `DATEPART(weekday, date)`    | `DAYOFWEEK(date)`                                                                        | 1=Sunday in T-SQL default locale (`@@DATEFIRST = 7`); 1=Sunday in DBSQL — equivalent **only when the source uses the default `@@DATEFIRST`**. If the source used `SET DATEFIRST` with a non-default value, day numbering differs and a manual offset is needed. |\n| `DATENAME(month, date)`      | `DATE_FORMAT(date, 'MMMM')`                                                              |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n\n### General scalar function mappings\n\nThese are non-date scalar functions frequently found alongside date code in T-SQL. Included here because there is no separate general-functions reference file.\n\n| T-SQL                    | DBSQL                                          | Drift warning                                                                                                                     |\n| ------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `IIF(cond, t, f)`        | `IF(cond, t, f)`                               |                                                                                                                                   |\n| `CONVERT(type, expr)`    | `CAST(expr AS type)`                           |                                                                                                                                   |\n| `CHOOSE(n, v1, v2, ...)` | `CASE n WHEN 1 THEN v1 WHEN 2 THEN v2 ... END` |                                                                                                                                   |\n| `NULLIF(a, b)`           | `NULLIF(a, b)`                                 | Same                                                                                                                              |\n| `COALESCE(a, b, ...)`    | `COALESCE(a, b, ...)`                          | Same                                                                                                                              |\n| `LOG(x)`                 | `LOG(x)`                                       | Identical in both dialects — no translation needed. `LN(x)` is an unambiguous alias you may use for clarity, but is not required. |\n| `LOG(x, base)`           | `LOG(base, x)`                                 | **Arguments reversed**: T-SQL is `LOG(x, base)`, DBSQL is `LOG(base, x)`                                                          |\n| `LOG10(x)`               | `LOG10(x)`                                     | Same in both                                                                                                                      |\n\n## SYNAPSE\n\nSame as MSSQL.\n"}}]);
